\title[The multiplicative law of the tokenizer tax]{The Multiplicative Law of the Tokenizer
Tax: Dilation Budgets, Characters of the Fragmentation Group, and the Non-Separability of
Multilingual Key-Cache Budget Tables}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{31 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Bounded key--value caches force a deployment question: how many attention keys must be
retained so that a fixed fraction $\tau$ of the attention mass survives? We show that for
multilingual workloads the answer is governed by a rigid algebraic law, and that the
additive ``tokenizer tax'' --- the extra keys a more finely tokenized language demands ---
is not an additive quantity at all. Starting from the power-law recall model
$\mathrm{deficit}(k)=Ak^{-a}$, we solve its gate exactly: the bar $\tau$ is met if and only
if $k \ge (A/(1-\tau))^{1/a}$. Writing the deficit amplitude as $A=A_0(\lambda C)^b$, with
$C$ the context length and $\lambda$ the fragmentation ratio of the language, the budget is
homogeneous of degree $1/a$ in the amplitude, so the language knob acts by multiplication
with an amplification factor $\chi(\lambda)=\lambda^{b/a}$ independent of the bar and of
the intrinsic difficulty, and $\chi$ is a character of the multiplicative group of
fragmentation ratios. Hence the \emph{multiplicative law}: the additive tax
$T(\lambda,C)=(\chi(\lambda)-1)\Bud(1,C)$ is a fixed multiple of the baseline, so between
two contexts the tax amplifies by exactly the baseline's acceleration. The transition from
a $+4$-key tax at short context to a $\ge+16$-key tax at context $4096$ is therefore the
already-measured $4\times$ baseline acceleration and not an independent parameter; the
hypotheses that the tax dissolves or persists as a constant offset are refuted, the latter
by an exact dichotomy. We prove that no separable budget table $f(C)+g(\lambda)$ exists for
a genuinely fragmenting language, that grid quantization cannot absorb the growth, and
that the entire development is model-independent: it follows from the notion of a
\emph{dilation budget}, whose response factor is \emph{forced} to be a character, and of
which a continuum Zipf attention profile --- with retention curve $(k/C)^{1-s}$ computed
from exact mass integrals --- is a second, parameter-disjoint model. Two parameter-free
falsifiable predictions follow.
\end{abstract}

