\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The deployment setting}
A transformer language model attends over its full context, but a deployed system rarely
stores the full attention state. Bounded key--value caches, sparse-attention schedulers and
retrieval-augmented pipelines all implement the same primitive: retain the $k$ most
important key positions and drop the rest. The engineering question is how large $k$ must
be for quality to be preserved, and the standard operationalization is a \emph{retention
gate}: the retained fraction of attention mass must reach a bar $\tau$, typically
$\tau=0.98$.

Two knobs move the answer. The first is the \emph{context length} $C$: as context grows,
attention mass spreads over more positions and a fixed top-$k$ slice recovers relatively
less. The second is the \emph{language}. A tokenizer trained predominantly on English
assigns single tokens to common English words and fragments other languages more finely.
Fixing a reference language, a target language has a \emph{fragmentation ratio}
$\lambda>1$: it spends $\lambda$ tokens per unit of reference content. From the point of
view of the attention mechanism, a document in the target language at context $C$ presents
the same workload as reference material at context $\lambda C$. This exchange principle
drives everything below.

\subsection{The measurement}
A fixed retrieval harness was applied to German prose at $C=4096$, with an exact gate and
three held-out windows, sweeping the key budget:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
$k$ & 24 & 32 & 40 & 48 & 56\\
retained & $0.953$ & $0.966$ & $0.973$ & $0.975$ & $0.976$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All five points fail the $0.98$ bar. At short contexts the same harness had established a
tokenizer penalty of about four extra keys relative to the reference language; at $4096$
that penalty is at least $+16$, a $4\times$ amplification coinciding exactly with the
measured acceleration of the baseline requirement. Three hypotheses were pre-registered:
\textbf{P1} (the tax compounds with the context acceleration), \textbf{P2} (the tax
dissolves at long context), \textbf{P3} (the tax persists as a fixed additive offset). P1
is confirmed; P2 and P3 are refuted. This paper replaces the table by an algebraic account
in which the confirmation and the refutations are theorems, the $4\times$ coincidence is an
identity, and the residual empirical content is a single exponent.

\section{The recall model}

Throughout, $k>0$ is a real key budget, $C>0$ a context length and $\tau<1$ a retention
bar.

\begin{definition}[Recall deficit, retained fraction]
For an amplitude $A$ and exponent $a$,
$\mathrm{deficit}(A,a,k)=A\,k^{-a}$ and
$\mathrm{retained}(A,a,k)=1-\mathrm{deficit}(A,a,k)$.
\end{definition}

Fitting this two-parameter family to the German table in log--log space gives
$a\approx 0.810$, $A\approx 0.582$, with residuals below $0.004$ at all five points.

\begin{definition}[Budget functional]
For $A>0$, $a>0$, $\tau<1$,
$\Bud(A,a,\tau)=\bigl(A/(1-\tau)\bigr)^{1/a}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Amplitude]
For a base difficulty $A_0$, context exponent $b$, fragmentation ratio $\lambda$ and
context $C$, $\Amp(A_0,b,\lambda,C)=A_0(\lambda C)^{b}$. We write
$\Bud(\lambda,C):=\Bud(\Amp(A_0,b,\lambda,C),a,\tau)$ and call $\Bud(1,C)$ the
\emph{baseline}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Amplification factor and tokenizer tax]
$\chi(\lambda)=\lambda^{b/a}$ and $T(\lambda,C)=\Bud(\lambda,C)-\Bud(1,C)$.
\end{definition}

For $A>0$, $k>0$ the deficit is positive; for $A>0$, $a>0$ it is strictly decreasing in
$k$, hence the retained fraction is strictly increasing in $k$; for $A>0$, $\tau<1$ the
budget is positive.

\section{The gate is exact}

\begin{theorem}[Exact gate]\label{thm:gate}
Let $A>0$, $a>0$, $\tau<1$, $k>0$. Then
\[
\tau \le \mathrm{retained}(A,a,k)\iff \Bud(A,a,\tau)\le k .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $\tau<1$ we have $1-\tau>0$ and $k^{a}>0$. The gate $\tau\le 1-Ak^{-a}$ is equivalent
to $Ak^{-a}\le 1-\tau$, i.e.\ to $A/(1-\tau)\le k^{a}$ after clearing positive
denominators. As $a>0$, the map $x\mapsto x^{1/a}$ is a strictly increasing bijection of
the positive reals with inverse $x\mapsto x^{a}$, so this is equivalent to
$(A/(1-\tau))^{1/a}\le k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One failure certifies all]\label{cor:allfail}
If $A>0$, $a>0$ and $\mathrm{retained}(A,a,56)<0.98$, then $\mathrm{retained}(A,a,k)<0.98$
for every $0<k\le 56$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strict monotonicity of the retained fraction in $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The budget was never reached]\label{cor:gt56}
Under the same hypotheses, $56<\Bud(A,a,0.98)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\Bud(A,a,0.98)\le 56$ then Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} at $k=56$ contradicts the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

With the fitted constants the budget at $\tau=0.98$ is near $64$ keys; the sweep stopped at
$56$ and could not have succeeded.

\section{Homogeneity and the character property}

\begin{theorem}[Homogeneity]\label{thm:hom}
For $A\ge 0$, $c\ge 0$, $\tau<1$: $\Bud(cA,a,\tau)=c^{1/a}\,\Bud(A,a,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplicativity of real powers on non-negative arguments.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Baseline form]\label{thm:baseline}
For $A_0\ge 0$, $\tau<1$, $C\ge 0$:
$\Bud(1,C)=\bigl(A_0/(1-\tau)\bigr)^{1/a}\,C^{\,b/a}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hom} with $c=C^{b}$ and use $(C^{b})^{1/a}=C^{b/a}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The language knob multiplies]\label{thm:lang}
For $A_0\ge0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda\ge0$, $C\ge0$:
$\Bud(\lambda,C)=\chi(\lambda)\,\Bud(1,C)$ with $\chi(\lambda)=\lambda^{b/a}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Amp(A_0,b,\lambda,C)=\lambda^{b}\Amp(A_0,b,1,C)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hom} with
$c=\lambda^{b}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The tax is a character]\label{thm:char}
The map $x\mapsto x^{b/a}$ is a monoid homomorphism of the non-negative reals under
multiplication: $\chi(1)=1$ and $\chi(\lambda_1\lambda_2)=\chi(\lambda_1)\chi(\lambda_2)$.
If moreover $a>0$, $b>0$ and $\lambda>1$ then $\chi(\lambda)>1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplicativity and normalization are properties of real powers; strictness holds because
$b/a>0$ and $x\mapsto\lambda^{x}$ is strictly increasing for $\lambda>1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universality of the amplification]\label{thm:univ}
For $A,B\ge0$, $\lambda\ge0$, $\tau_1,\tau_2<1$:
\[
\Bud(\lambda^{b}A,a,\tau_1)\,\Bud(B,a,\tau_2)=\Bud(A,a,\tau_1)\,\Bud(\lambda^{b}B,a,\tau_2).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides equal $\lambda^{b/a}\Bud(A,a,\tau_1)\Bud(B,a,\tau_2)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:hom}.
\end{proof}

Thus the amplification depends neither on the retention bar nor on the intrinsic difficulty
of the workload: laboratories using different bars and corpora measure the same
$\chi(\lambda)$.

\section{The multiplicative law}

\begin{theorem}[Tax as a multiple of the baseline]\label{thm:taxmult}
For $A_0\ge0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda\ge0$, $C\ge0$:
$T(\lambda,C)=\bigl(\chi(\lambda)-1\bigr)\Bud(1,C)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The multiplicative law]\label{thm:law}
For $A_0\ge0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda\ge0$, $C_1,C_2\ge0$:
\[
T(\lambda,C_2)\,\Bud(1,C_1)=T(\lambda,C_1)\,\Bud(1,C_2).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:taxmult} both sides equal
$(\chi(\lambda)-1)\Bud(1,C_1)\Bud(1,C_2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Acceleration form]\label{thm:accel}
If $\Bud(1,C_2)=\rho\,\Bud(1,C_1)$ then $T(\lambda,C_2)=\rho\,T(\lambda,C_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[$+4\mapsto+16$]\label{cor:four}
If the baseline quadruples between $C_1$ and $C_2$ and the short-context tax is $4$ keys,
then the long-context tax is exactly $16$ keys.
\end{corollary}

The hypotheses are satisfiable, so the corollary is not vacuous: with
$A_0=b=a=1$, $\tau=1/2$, $\lambda=3$, $C_1=1$, $C_2=4$ one computes that the baseline
quadruples, $T(3,1)=4$ and $T(3,4)=16$.

\begin{theorem}[P2 refuted: the tax is unbounded]\label{thm:unbounded}
If $A_0>0$, $a>0$, $b>0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda>1$, then for every $M\in\mathbb{R}$ there is
$C>0$ with $T(\lambda,C)>M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$T(\lambda,C)=\kappa\beta C^{b/a}$ with $\kappa=\chi(\lambda)-1>0$ and $\beta>0$ by
Theorems~\ref{thm:baseline},~\ref{thm:char},~\ref{thm:taxmult}; since $b/a>0$ the right
side is unbounded in $C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[P3 refuted: the tax is not constant]\label{thm:notconst}
Under the same hypotheses there is no $t$ with $T(\lambda,C)=t$ for all $C>0$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Sub-linearity and the explosion}

\begin{theorem}[The data force a sub-linear exponent]\label{thm:sublinear}
If $\mathrm{deficit}(A,a,24)=0.047$ and $\mathrm{deficit}(A,a,56)=0.024$ then $a<1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Dividing gives $(56/24)^{a}=0.047/0.024\approx 1.958$. If $a\ge1$ then, since the base
$56/24\approx 2.333$ exceeds $1$, $(56/24)^{a}\ge 56/24>1.958$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Super-linear response]\label{thm:superlin}
If $A>0$, $0<a<1$, $\tau<1$, $c>1$ then $\Bud(cA,a,\tau)>c\,\Bud(A,a,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The left side is $c^{1/a}\Bud(A,a,\tau)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:hom}, and $c^{1/a}>c$ because
$1/a>1$ and $c>1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The amplification exceeds the token-count penalty]\label{thm:exceeds}
If $0<a<1$, $b>0$, $\lambda>1$, then $\lambda^{b}<\chi(\lambda)=\lambda^{b/a}$.
\end{theorem}

A naive accounting charges a language $\lambda^{b}$, exactly for the extra tokens it
spends. The true charge is strictly larger; the compounding rate is $1/a$.

\section{Rigidity: no budget table is separable}

\begin{theorem}[Language--context exchange symmetry]\label{thm:exchange}
$\Bud(\lambda,C)=\Bud(1,\lambda C)$ for all parameters.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The amplitude depends on $\lambda$ and $C$ only through $\lambda C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Affine log-law with a single slope]\label{thm:log}
For $A_0>0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda>0$, $C>0$:
\[
\log\Bud(\lambda,C)=\frac1a\log\frac{A_0}{1-\tau}+\frac{b}{a}\log\lambda+\frac{b}{a}\log C .
\]
\end{theorem}

Budgets are additive in log-space, with the \emph{same} coefficient on the language and
context axes --- the exchange symmetry made quantitative. This is the only additive shape
available:

\begin{theorem}[No additive budget table]\label{thm:nosep}
Let $A_0>0$, $a>0$, $b>0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda>0$. If there are functions $f,g$ with
$\Bud(l,C)=f(C)+g(l)$ for all $l,C>0$, then $\chi(\lambda)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Separability makes $\Bud(\lambda,C)-\Bud(1,C)=g(\lambda)-g(1)$ independent of $C$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:taxmult} that difference is $(\chi(\lambda)-1)\Bud(1,C)$, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:baseline} the baseline takes two distinct values as $C$ varies (since
$b/a>0$). Evaluating there forces $\chi(\lambda)-1=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A genuine language has no table]\label{cor:notable}
If $A_0>0$, $a>0$, $b>0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda>1$, there exist no $f,g$ with
$\Bud(l,C)=f(C)+g(l)$ for all positive $l,C$.
\end{corollary}

The universal practice of sizing caches from a table with a context row and a language
column is not merely inaccurate at the margins; the functional form it presupposes is
impossible. A correct table must carry a language\,$\times$\,context interaction term.

\subsection{Quantization does not save you}

\begin{definition}[Grid steps]
For grid $g$ and demand $x$, $\mathrm{steps}(g,x)=\lceil x/g\rceil$; it is monotone in $x$
for $g>0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Quadrupling costs blocks]\label{thm:steps}
For all $g$ and $x$: $4\,\mathrm{steps}(g,x)-3\le \mathrm{steps}(g,4x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $y=x/g$: $\lceil y\rceil<y+1$ gives $4\lceil y\rceil<4y+4\le\lceil 4y\rceil+4$, and
integrality upgrades this to $4\lceil y\rceil\le\lceil 4y\rceil+3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
On a grid of $4$, a real tax of $4$ costs one block and a real tax of $16$ costs four: the
amplification passes through the quantizer intact.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[No finite block surcharge is safe]\label{thm:stepunb}
If $A_0>0$, $a>0$, $b>0$, $\tau<1$, $\lambda>1$ and $g>0$, then for every integer $N$
there is $C>0$ with $N\le\mathrm{steps}(g,T(\lambda,C))$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Model independence: dilation budgets}

\begin{definition}[Dilation budget]\label{def:dil}
A \emph{dilation budget} consists of a budget function $\Bud:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ of
the context length and a response factor $\chi:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ such that
$\Bud(C)>0$ for all $C>0$, and $\Bud(uC)=\chi(u)\Bud(C)$ for all $u,C>0$. Its
\emph{language tax} is $T(\lambda,C)=\Bud(\lambda C)-\Bud(C)$.
\end{definition}

Nothing is assumed about $\chi$ beyond its existence. Multiplicativity is a theorem.

\begin{theorem}[Normalization]\label{thm:chione}
In any dilation budget, $\chi(1)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The response factor is forced to be a character]\label{thm:chimul}
In any dilation budget, $\chi(uv)=\chi(u)\chi(v)$ for $u,v>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compute $\Bud(u(v\cdot1))$ in two ways: applying the dilation law twice gives
$\chi(u)\chi(v)\Bud(1)$, applying it once to $uv$ gives $\chi(uv)\Bud(1)$; cancel the
positive $\Bud(1)$.
\end{proof}

Multiplicativity of the tokenizer tax is therefore associativity of dilation in disguise.

\begin{theorem}[Tax as a multiple of the baseline, abstractly]\label{thm:abstax}
$T(\lambda,C)=(\chi(\lambda)-1)\Bud(C)$ for $\lambda,C>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The multiplicative law, structurally]\label{thm:abslaw}
In any dilation budget, $T(\lambda,C_2)\Bud(C_1)=T(\lambda,C_1)\Bud(C_2)$; and if
$\Bud(C_2)=\rho\Bud(C_1)$ then $T(\lambda,C_2)=\rho\,T(\lambda,C_1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact dichotomy for a constant tax]\label{thm:dich}
In any dilation budget, for $\lambda>0$ and $C_1,C_2>0$,
\[
T(\lambda,C_1)=T(\lambda,C_2)\iff \bigl(\chi(\lambda)=1 \ \text{ or } \ \Bud(C_1)=\Bud(C_2)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The equation is $(\chi(\lambda)-1)(\Bud(C_1)-\Bud(C_2))=0$; a product of reals vanishes iff
a factor does.
\end{proof}

So P3 has no room: a context-independent tax requires either a free language or a frozen
baseline.

\subsection{Model 1: the power-law recall model}

\begin{proposition}
For $A_0>0$, $\tau<1$, the pair $\Bud(C)=(A_0/(1-\tau))^{1/a}C^{b/a}$, $\chi(u)=u^{b/a}$ is
a dilation budget; on non-negative contexts its budget function is the power-law budget at
fragmentation ratio $1$, and its response factor is the amplification factor.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Model 2: a continuum Zipf attention profile}

Suppose attention mass over a context of length $C$ is described by the continuum density
$x\mapsto x^{-s}$ on $(0,C]$, with $s<1$ (needed for integrability at the origin).

\begin{theorem}[Total mass]\label{thm:mass}
For $s<1$: $\displaystyle\int_0^{C}x^{-s}\,dx=\frac{C^{\,1-s}}{1-s}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Antidifferentiate $x^{-s}$ to $x^{-s+1}/(-s+1)$, valid since $-s>-1$; the boundary term at
$0$ vanishes because $1-s>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Zipf retention curve, derived]\label{thm:zipfret}
For $s<1$, $k>0$, $C>0$, the fraction of attention mass recovered by the top $k$ positions
is
\[
\frac{\int_0^{k}x^{-s}\,dx}{\int_0^{C}x^{-s}\,dx}=\Bigl(\frac{k}{C}\Bigr)^{1-s}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}
The Zipf budget is $\Bz(C)=\tau^{1/(1-s)}\,C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The Zipf gate is exact]\label{thm:zipfgate}
For $s<1$, $\tau>0$, $k>0$, $C>0$:
$\tau\le (k/C)^{1-s}\iff \Bz(C)\le k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Raise both sides to the power $1/(1-s)>0$, a strictly increasing operation on positives, to
get $\tau^{1/(1-s)}\le k/C$, and clear the positive $C$; reverse by raising to $1-s>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The Zipf model is a dilation budget]\label{thm:zipfdil}
For $\tau>0$, the pair $\Bz$, $\chi_{\mathrm{Z}}(u)=u$ is a dilation budget. Hence
$T_{\mathrm{Z}}(\lambda,C)=(\lambda-1)\Bz(C)$ and the multiplicative law holds: a $4\times$
longer context carries a $4\times$ larger tokenizer tax.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{What is structural and what is measurable}

\begin{theorem}[The characters agree iff $b=a$]\label{thm:chieq}
For $a>0$ and $\lambda>1$: $\lambda^{b/a}=\lambda\iff b=a$.
\end{theorem}

The multiplicative law is common to every dilation budget and cannot distinguish models.
The exponent is where the empirical content sits: the observed $4\times$ amplification at
$C=4096$ measures $b/a$, and nothing else.

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity of the character]\label{thm:mono}
If $a>0$, $b>0$ and $0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2$ then $\chi(\lambda_1)<\chi(\lambda_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Two parameter-free predictions}

Both hold in every dilation budget, hence in both models, and neither contains a fitted
quantity.

\begin{prediction}[The language ratio is a context invariant]\label{pred:ratio}
For $\lambda_1,\lambda_2>0$ and $C_1,C_2>0$,
\[
T(\lambda_1,C_1)\,T(\lambda_2,C_2)=T(\lambda_1,C_2)\,T(\lambda_2,C_1).
\]
\end{prediction}

Each language's tax diverges with context, but the ratio of two languages' taxes is the
same at every context length; measuring French and German at two contexts tests this with
no free parameter.

\begin{prediction}[Rankings never cross]\label{pred:order}
If $\chi(\lambda_1)\le\chi(\lambda_2)$ then $T(\lambda_1,C)\le T(\lambda_2,C)$ for every
$C>0$.
\end{prediction}

In the power-law model Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} decides the hypothesis from the fragmentation
ratios alone: if $1<\lambda_{\mathrm{fr}}<\lambda_{\mathrm{de}}$ then French must sit
strictly between the reference baseline and the German curve at every context. An observed
crossing would refute the dilation structure itself.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Exact budget from a measured sweep.} Given $(k_i,r_i)_{i=1}^{n}$, regress
$\log(1-r_i)$ on $\log k_i$ to obtain slope $-a$ and intercept $\log A$; return
$(A/(1-\tau))^{1/a}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} this is the exact gate threshold for the
fitted model. Cost $O(n)$.

\textbf{B. Tax projection across contexts.} Given a tax $T_1$ at $C_1$ and the baselines at
$C_1,C_2$, return $T_2=T_1\Bud(1,C_2)/\Bud(1,C_1)$; exact in any dilation budget by
Theorem~\ref{thm:law}, and the required input is a reference-language measurement. Cost
$O(1)$. With $\rho=4$, $T_1=4$ it returns $16$.

\textbf{C. Interaction-aware budget table.} Store the three log-space coefficients of
Theorem~\ref{thm:log} and evaluate
$\Bud(\lambda,C)=\exp(c_0+\tfrac{b}{a}\log\lambda+\tfrac{b}{a}\log C)$, then quantize by
$\lceil\cdot/g\rceil$; Theorems~\ref{thm:steps} and~\ref{thm:stepunb} guarantee the
quantized table inherits the amplification. Cost $O(1)$ per lookup.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the experiment actually measured}
The strongest reading of the cell is negative: the sweep never reached the budget. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:allfail} the five failures are one failure, and by
Corollary~\ref{cor:gt56} the true requirement exceeded $56$; with the fitted constants it
is near $64$. The positive content is the ratio: the tax multiplied by the same factor as
the baseline, as Theorem~\ref{thm:law} requires. The cell is therefore a successful
consistency check of the dilation structure and a measurement of the exponent $b/a$.

\subsection{Why the additive intuition persists}
Additive intuition is correct at fixed context: there $T(\lambda,C)$ really is a constant.
The error appears when the surcharge is carried across contexts --- exactly what happens
when a short-context benchmark sizes a long-context deployment. Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} says
there is no intermediate regime.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}
The empirical base is one language pair, three held-out windows, one context length and one
model scale, so $b/a$ rests on that cell. The theory's contribution is that this single
number is all there is to measure: the shape of the language\,$\times$\,context interaction
is fixed by the dilation structure and only its slope is free. A second idealization is
that a language enters only through an effective context stretch $\lambda C$; this treats
fragmentation as uniform and ignores language-specific changes in the \emph{shape} of the
attention distribution. That the Zipf model preserves the law despite a wholly different
shape is reassuring, but a language whose shape changed with context would fall outside the
framework, and Prediction~\ref{pred:order} is the sharpest available test.

\subsection{Deployment consequences}
(i) A short-context tokenizer benchmark does not transfer: multiply the measured surcharge
by the baseline acceleration. (ii) Budget tables must carry an interaction term
(Corollary~\ref{cor:notable}) or be stored in log-space (Theorem~\ref{thm:log}). (iii)
Block quantization is not a mitigation (Theorems~\ref{thm:steps},~\ref{thm:stepunb}).
Non-English agentic workloads face disproportionately growing cache costs as context
extends past roughly two thousand tokens, at a rate $1/a$ times worse than a token-count
accounting suggests (Theorem~\ref{thm:exceeds}).

\section{Future work}

Experimentally: a French cell at $4096$ (does a romance language behave like a germanic
one?), more languages at $4096$, a larger non-English model at the same context, and a
larger model scale. Prediction~\ref{pred:ratio} becomes testable with two languages at two
contexts, Prediction~\ref{pred:order} with three languages at one.

Theoretically, the natural next step is a rigidity theorem for the response factor itself:
a budget functional monotone in the context and covariant under the two commuting dilations
appears to have no freedom left, its response factor being forced to be a continuous
character of the positive reals, hence a pure power $u\mapsto u^{\theta}$ --- in the spirit
of the classical regularity solutions of the Cauchy functional equation. If so, the entire
multilingual budget theory collapses to a single real number per language. Also open:
whether the exchange principle survives non-uniform fragmentation; whether $b$ and $a$ can
be identified separately (Theorem~\ref{thm:chieq} shows only their ratio enters the
character, so a second observable is required); and whether the same structure governs
other bounded-memory primitives such as sparse-attention schedules, retrieval-chunk budgets
and speculative-decoding drafts.

\section{Conclusion}

The tokenizer tax is a character. Because a language shift acts on the workload as a
dilation of the context, and because dilations compose, the multiplicative response factor
is forced to be a homomorphism and the additive tax is a fixed multiple of the baseline.
The observed $+4\mapsto+16$ transition is then an identity rather than a discovery; the
hypotheses that the tax dissolves or persists at a fixed offset are refuted with an exact
dichotomy; additive budget tables are impossible; grid quantization does not help. The law
survives replacing the recall model wholesale. What remains empirical is one exponent.
Everything else is algebra.

\end{document}

